[% PageVersion = 'Documentation/system/DEPLOY_WORKFLOW.tt,v 1.0 2026/07/31 Shanta Exp ' %]

<h1 id="deploy_top">Canonical Deploy Workflow (Production)</h1>

<div class="container warning">
    <div class="container-header">Single Source of Truth</div>
    <div class="container-content">
        <div class="container-content-inner">
            <p><strong>This is the ONLY supported procedure for getting new code onto production1.</strong>
            Every deploy goes through these three steps, in this order, all initiated from the
            <strong>workstation</strong> web UI. There is no other path.</p>
            <p>The previous document <code>REBUILD_AND_DEPLOY_INSTRUCTIONS.tt</code> described a different,
            outdated flow (building inside the production container, <code>docker-compose.server.yml</code>)
            and is <strong>superseded</strong> — do not follow it.</p>
            <p><strong>Production1 is image-only (2026-08-24).</strong> There is no application source on
            .126 that may be used. <strong>No git pull. No docker build. No host Starman.
            No workstation containers</strong> (<code>comserv2-web</code>, <code>comserv-web-dev</code>,
            <code>comserv-web-staging</code>). The only web process is <code>comserv2-web-prod</code>
            (plus redis and config-db). Monitor skips git and lib-sync; it only health-checks and
            purges siblings. Build scripts on the host were moved to
            <code>/opt/comserv/REMOVED-DO-NOT-RUN/</code>. The git <code>origin</code> remote was
            removed so a pull cannot overwrite this.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="why">Why this exists</h2>
<p>Production1 is a <strong>Docker container</strong>. You cannot build or run Docker commands from inside it,
and the app explicitly blocks Docker management when reached from a container. All image building happens on
the <strong>workstation</strong> (which has the source, the Docker daemon, and registry credentials). The
container on production1 only ever receives a pre-built image pulled from Docker Hub.</p>

<p>The recurring failure mode was: build + push succeeded, but the <em>pull onto production1</em> step was
skipped or forgotten, so the running container kept serving the old image. This workflow makes the pull an
explicit, required final step, and verification (below) catches any mismatch before customers see it.</p>

<h2 id="prereqs">Prerequisites</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Code committed and pushed to <code>main</code> on the workstation (the build reads <code>git rev-parse HEAD</code>).</li>
    <li>Workstation has Docker Hub credentials configured (registry login works during push).</li>
    <li>You are on the <strong>workstation</strong> web UI at <code>/admin/docker-containers</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="steps">The three steps</h2>

<h3>Step 1 — Build on workstation</h3>
<p>In the Docker Servers page, set the <strong>Target</strong> to the workstation (or use the inline
<em>Rebuild</em> control) and trigger a build of <code>comserv-web-prod</code>.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>No Cache is ON by default.</strong> A clean rebuild avoids the classic "built but nothing
        changed" failure where a stale layer masks your edits. Uncheck only if you have a specific reason.</li>
    <li>The build regenerates <code>version.json</code> from the current git commit + build timestamp, so the
        resulting image is self-identifying.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Step 2 — Push the image to Docker Hub</h3>
<p>Use <strong>Build &amp; Push only</strong> mode (or the Full Rebuild which includes this). This tags the
image as <code>shantamcsbain/comserv-web-prod:latest</code> and pushes it to Docker Hub.</p>
<ul>
    <li>The build explicitly targets the Dockerfile <code>production</code> stage and regenerates
        <code>version.json</code> immediately before building.</li>
    <li>Do not trust a generic push-success line. The deploy engine compares the newly built local image ID
        with Docker Hub's manifest <em>config digest</em>. It reports success only when they are identical.</li>
    <li>Do NOT consider the deploy done at this point — production1 still runs the old image.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Step 3 — Pull &amp; Deploy onto production1</h3>
<p>On the same Docker Servers page, set the <strong>Target</strong> to
<strong>Production 1 (192.168.1.126)</strong>, select mode
<strong>Pull &amp; Deploy (from registry)</strong>, and start it. This:</p>
<ol>
    <li>Pulls <code>shantamcsbain/comserv-web-prod:latest</code> from Docker Hub onto production1.</li>
    <li>Verifies production1's pulled image ID equals Docker Hub's config digest; a mismatch leaves production untouched.</li>
    <li>Creates/verifies the portable Docker NFS volume
        <code>comserv2_nfs_data_nfs</code> (<code>192.168.1.175:/mnt/data</code>, nfsvers=3)
        and proves an STL is visible before renaming the live container.</li>
    <li>Backs up the current container (keep 3 dated <code>bk-*</code>).</li>
    <li>Recreates <code>comserv2-web-prod</code> from the verified image with 8 Starman workers
        and <code>--max-requests 1000</code>.</li>
    <li>Runs the health-check loop (up to 90s). On failure it auto-rolls back to the backup.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Alternative:</strong> the <strong>Full Rebuild</strong> mode combines Steps 1–3 in one shot
(build → push → pull → recreate). Use it when you want a single click; use the split steps when you want to
verify the pushed image before touching production.</p>

<h2 id="verify">Verification (mandatory)</h2>
<p>After Step 3, confirm production1 is actually running the new code. The app footer reports the build
identity pulled from <code>version.json</code>:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Open the production site (computersystemconsulting.ca or the production1 LAN URL).</li>
    <li>Check the footer <strong>Build</strong> date and <strong>Git</strong> commit hash.</li>
    <li>It must match the commit you just built (e.g. <code>main@&lt;new_hash&gt;</code>) and a current
        timestamp — <strong>not</strong> an old date or an old hash.</li>
    <li>If the footer still shows the prior build, the pull did not take effect: re-run Step 3 and watch the
        log for pull errors or a health-check rollback.</li>
</ul>
<p>This single check is what catches the "old server" problem before customers hit it.</p>

<h2 id="current_state">Current operational state (2026-08-23)</h2>
<p>All dashboard buttons run <code>Comserv/script/deploy.sh</code> from the workstation app.
They do <strong>not</strong> SSH to the workstation (that hop was a 2026-08-23 failure).
They do <strong>not</strong> all do the same thing. The button you click is the contract:</p>
<table class="diagnostic-table">
    <tbody>
        <tr><th>UI action</th><th>What actually runs</th><th>Production1</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Rebuild, target = workstation</td>
            <td><code>deploy.sh --local-rebuild</code>: build on this machine, rename live to <code>bk-*</code>, start the new local image. <strong>No push. No remote hop.</strong></td>
            <td>Not touched. Log must say &quot;Production was NOT updated.&quot;</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Build &amp; Push</td>
            <td><code>deploy.sh --build-push</code>: build + push <code>shantamcsbain/comserv-web-prod:latest</code>. Running container is not started or replaced.</td>
            <td>Not touched. Pushing <code>:latest</code> does not move prod.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Pull &amp; Deploy, target = Production 1</td>
            <td><code>deploy.sh --pull-deploy 192.168.1.126</code>: rename live on .126, <code>docker pull</code>, <code>docker run</code> the image (no compose, no host source tree), health gate, rollback on fail.</td>
            <td>This is the only step that updates prod.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Rebuild, target = Production 1</td>
            <td><code>deploy.sh --deploy-to-node 192.168.1.126</code>: workstation build+push, then the production pull-run above.</td>
            <td>Updated only if the remote pull-run succeeds.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>Key invariants:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Never build on production1.</strong> <code>is_production_host()</code> refuses compose build there.</li>
    <li><strong>Never claim multi-node success after a local rebuild.</strong> That lie (2026-08-23) made a workstation Rebuild look like prod had been pulled. It had not.</li>
    <li><strong>Streaming:</strong> the child writes live to <code>/tmp/comserv_deploy.log</code> via <code>script -f -a</code>. The Docker page paints the whole log every 1.5s. Hard-refresh the page so the JS loads.</li>
    <li><strong>Health:</strong> <code>/health</code> is a cheap JSON liveness check. Catalyst path has no leading slash; <code>Root.pm</code> skips <code>auto</code>/<code>begin</code> on <code>health</code>. Compose <code>start_period</code> is 60s (not 10s).</li>
    <li><strong>Volumes:</strong> theme/cache/logs stay named local volumes. Shared files are
        <code>comserv2_nfs_data_nfs</code> — a Docker-managed NFS volume, not a host bind
        and not the empty leftover <code>comserv2_nfs_data</code> local volume.</li>
    <li><strong>Workers:</strong> production Starman default is 8, recycled after 1000 requests.</li>
    <li><strong>No auto-commit.</strong> Code ships inside the image. Footer <code>+local</code> means uncommitted working-tree files were baked.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>See changelog entries <code>2026-08-23-deploy-pull-only-image-css</code>,
<code>2026-08-23-workstation-rebuild-honest</code>,
<code>2026-08-25-prod-image-artifact-verification</code>,
and <code>2026-08-25-nfs-docker-volume</code>.</em></p>

<h2 id="trouble">Troubleshooting</h2>
<table class="diagnostic-table">
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Footer still shows old build</strong></td>
            <td>Pull &amp; Deploy did not update the image. Re-run Step 3; check the deploy log for
                <code>pull</code> errors or a health-check failure that triggered rollback.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Container "unhealthy" after deploy</strong></td>
            <td>New code failed the health check &rarr; auto-rolled back to the previous image. Read the container
                logs (<code>docker logs comserv2-web-prod</code>) for the boot error. Fix, then redeploy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>web / web-dev / web-staging keep coming back on production1</strong></td>
            <td>Workstation siblings, same image as prod. A bare <code>compose up -d</code> creates them and
                <code>web</code> steals :5000. Do not keep deleting them by hand. Monitor runs
                <code>purge_workstation_web_siblings</code> every 10 min on .126 (removes those three names only,
                restarts web-prod). From the workstation Docker page, target Production 1 — running siblings have
                <strong>Remove from prod</strong>. Keep redis and config-db. An image rebuild alone does not stop this.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>"Built but nothing changed"</strong></td>
            <td>Stale Docker layer. No Cache is default-on now; if you unchecked it, re-run with it checked.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cannot deploy from inside production1</strong></td>
            <td>Expected. Production1 is a container; Docker management is blocked there. Always drive deploys
                from the workstation UI.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong><code>undefined volume comserv2_cache: invalid compose project</code></strong></td>
            <td>The deploy ran <code>docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml</code> without the base file. Fixed in
                <code>deploy.sh</code> via <code>COMPOSE_ARGS</code> merge; if you run compose manually on prod, always include
                <code>-f docker-compose.yml</code> too.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Production site has no CSS / unstyled</strong></td>
            <td>The running container is stale (old empty <code>/root/static</code> bind). Pull + recreate from the fixed image
                (Step 3 / Rebuild) so the baked <code>root/static/css</code> tree is used. Do not bind a host <code>/root/static</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Rebuild said SUCCESS but production1 is still the old image</strong></td>
            <td>Either the target was workstation (local rebuild never updates prod), or Build &amp; Push
                did not publish the local image. The log must show
                <code>Pushed and verified</code> with matching local ID and registry config digest.
                A generic “Image is up to date” pull of yesterday’s digest is a failed publish, not a failed pull.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Print-queue STL: File not found on server /data/nfs/...</strong></td>
            <td>The file is on NFS; the container’s <code>/data/nfs</code> is the empty local
                <code>comserv2_nfs_data</code> volume. Recreate that host’s
                <code>comserv2-web-prod</code> so it mounts <code>comserv2_nfs_data_nfs</code>.
                Confirm with <code>docker exec comserv2-web-prod df -T /data/nfs</code>
                (must say <code>nfs</code>) and <code>ls</code> the stored path.
                Do not bind <code>/home/ubuntu/nfs</code> — that is host-specific.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Workstation Docker card shows an older image than production1</strong></td>
            <td>Expected after Build &amp; Push / Pull &amp; Deploy. Those do not recreate the
                workstation container. Switch the Docker page target to Production 1 to see prod.
                Recreate workstation Docker separately if it must run the same image/volume.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>No streaming / window frozen for minutes</strong></td>
            <td>Hard-refresh the Docker page. Output is <code>/tmp/comserv_deploy.log</code>; the poller paints the full file. If :3001 is down (NetworkError), the poll cannot run — the build may still be writing the log.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Volumes panel shows ~20 instead of ~12</strong></td>
            <td>Old compose-prefixed leftovers. The panel now lists only exact <code>comserv2_*</code> names. Prefixed copies were removed 2026-08-23.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Deploy aborts with <code>command not found</code> / <code>... not found. Aborting.</code></strong></td>
            <td>Stale <code>deploy.sh</code> on the server. Ensure <code>/opt/comserv/Comserv/script/deploy.sh</code> (and
                <code>/opt/comserv/Comserv/deploy.sh</code>) is the current copy; the dashboard self-updates it on each run.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="future">Reserved / Future</h2>
<p>The code retains an internal path that triggers a deploy from inside a production container (it SSHes back to
the workstation to perform the build). This is <strong>not part of the supported workflow today</strong> and is
kept intentionally: as the AI code-editor capability matures, managing workstation build/ deploy actions
remotely from the Docker UI may become desirable. Until then, follow Steps 1–3 above and ignore that path.</p>

<div style="margin-top: 40px; padding: 20px; background-color: var(--bg-secondary, #f5f5f5); border-radius: 4px;">
